   184	| Reclaim   | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`       | `:dao.lease/cause` — silence, release, cap, or policy                  |
   185	
   186	`:dao.lease/subject` names **what is leased** — the resource, in whatever plain
   187	data the domain uses to denote it. `:dao.lease/holder` names **to whom** —
   188	the author whose renewals count, in whatever form the judged medium's
   189	attribution takes. The grant carries both outright rather than referring back
   190	to a proposal, because a proposal may not exist, and because a reader of the
   191	grant alone must be able to say what was granted and to whom.
   192	
   193	Without the holder the attribution rules below are unenforceable in the only
   194	way that matters: a judge that cannot map lease to holder cannot reject a
   195	renewal from the wrong author, so any co-writer that learns a lease id can keep
   196	a dead holder's tenure alive. An unsolicited grant has no proposal whose
   197	authorship could be read back, so nothing else supplies it.
   198	
   199	`:dao.lease/lease` is the lease's identity, and **the grantor mints it**: it
   200	appears first on the grant, and every later fact about the lease carries it. A
   201	proposal cannot carry one, because a lease does not exist until it is granted.
   202	A proposal carries `:dao.lease/proposal` instead — its own identity, minted by
   203	the holder — which a grant or refusal echoes so a holder can tell which of its
   204	asks was answered. Exactly one of the two may answer a proposal; a second
   205	answer, of either kind, is a defective fact and changes nothing.
   206	
   207	Minting the lease id at the grantor also settles uniqueness, since every id a
   208	judge reads is one it issued. Proposal ids need no such guarantee: a judge that
   209	cannot tell two proposals apart has only failed to answer an ask, which it owed
   210	nobody.

