   212	`:dao.lease/duration` is a plain-data quantity carrying its own unit: a
   213	single-entry map from a unit keyword to a non-negative integer magnitude, not a
   214	bare number whose unit lives in a convention. One unit is enough — a
   215	composition that wants finer granularity chooses a finer unit — and a value with
   216	zero or several entries, a non-integer magnitude, or a unit the judge does not
   217	know is a defective fact. Comparison normalizes to the finer of the two units;
   218	a judge that cannot normalize two units has read units it does not know, which
   219	is the defective case again. Durations are positive: absent, zero, or negative
   220	is not a grant and establishes nothing.
   221	
   222	`:dao.lease/max` and a judge's tolerance use the same representation, and
   223	tolerance is non-negative. The lapse comparison is strict, so an interval
   224	exactly equal to duration plus tolerance has not yet lapsed. Tolerance applies
   225	to the duration and **not** to the cap: tolerance absorbs flight time on
   226	evidence, while the cap is measured from the grantor's own observation of its
   227	own grant, where nothing was in flight.
   228	
   229	A cap below the duration is permitted, not defective — it simply means the cap
   230	is the bound that will end the tenure, and a lease lapses at whichever bound is
   231	reached first. Sizing is a composition's business, and the vocabulary refuses
   232	only what it cannot interpret.
   233	
   234	**Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot renew itself
   235	into tenure by asserting one, and a replayed or delayed holder fact establishes
   236	nothing. A proposal may carry a desired duration; it is advisory, exactly as a
   237	patch's replacement block is advisory until the VM accepts it. A grantor may
   238	grant less than was asked and owes no explanation.
   239	
   240	**A proposal creates no state.** An unanswered proposal is not pending and is
   241	not queued — it is simply not a lease. Nothing exists until `:accepted`, and a
   242	reader that finds a proposal with no acceptance has found a lease that was
   243	never granted. The statuses are a record of speech acts, not a state machine: a
   244	proposal is something someone said, not something the system holds.
   245	
   246	Three consequences follow, and each closes a question the vocabulary would
   247	otherwise leave open. A grantor **owes no answer** to a proposal; silence is
   248	its own sufficient response. `:dao.lease/rejected` is therefore courtesy in
   249	exactly the way `:dao.lease/released` is — it buys the holder a definitive
   250	negative it was never owed, and a system in which no refusal is ever appended
   251	is fully correct. And a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by
   252	**nothing in this vocabulary**: it is an ordinary unanswered request, and how
   253	long to wait before proposing again is the holder's own policy. A proposal that
   254	was answered — accepted or refused — has spent its identity; renegotiating is a
   255	new proposal with a new proposal id, since each status is authored exactly once.
   256	
   257	A grantor may also grant **unsolicited** — a lease whose first fact is
   258	`:accepted` — because the canonical case, a server leasing a per-connection
   259	composition, asks nobody first.
   260	
   261	Either way the grant must **reach the holder**, because the holder's own bound
   262	is measured from observing it: a holder that has not observed its grant holds
   263	nothing and must not act. Delivery is carriage, not a second authoring — the
   264	grantor authors `:accepted` once, on its own log, and the composition carries
   265	that same fact to the holder's medium as ordinary payload. The one fact that
   266	does not travel is `:lapsed`, for the reason given under *Lapse is a
   267	conclusion*.
   268	
   269	`:dao.lease/released` is the holder saying it is done. It is evidence, not a
   270	request and not an act: the grantor still performs the reclaim, on its own
   271	schedule, exactly as it would on a lapse. What release buys is promptness — the
   272	grantor need not wait out a duration of silence it already knows the reason
   273	for. A release from anyone but the lease's holder is not a release; a release
   274	after `:lapsed` is a late fact about a tenure already ended and changes nothing;
   275	and where release and lapse race, whichever the grantor acts on first is the
   276	one that terminates the lease, since both lead to the same idempotent reclaim.
   277	
   278	`:dao.lease/lapsed` is the reclaim record; its ordering with the act of
   279	reclaiming is specified under *Judging lapse*.
   280	
   281	There is no `:rolled-back`. The precedent's word is **renamed rather than
   282	omitted**, and the reason is connotation: rollback suggests undoing an
   283	application and restoring the world as if it had not happened, while a lapse
   284	ends a tenure whose entire past remains valid and undoes nothing. `:released`
   285	has no patch analogue at all, because an unapplied patch costs nothing while a
   286	holder done early saves the grantor up to one duration of waiting.
   287	
   288	## Renewal is evidence, not negotiation
   289	
   290	A renewal is the trace half:
   291	
   292	```clojure
   293	{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal
   294	 :dao.lease/lease <id>}
   295	```
   296	
   297	It carries no time and proposes nothing. It says one thing — *I still want
   298	this* — and the fact that it was appended, together with when it was observed,
   299	is its entire content. A renewal that asked for a new duration would be
   300	negotiating; renegotiation is a new proposal, not a fat renewal.
   301	
   302	**A renewal is a fact, not a claim on the future** — but the grant is a claim
   303	about tolerance, and the grantor keeps it. A grantor honours eligible renewals
   304	for the granted duration: that is what makes the duration mean anything, and
   305	without it the holder's own bound would be advice about a number nobody
   306	respects. What the grantor retains is the right to end a lease for reasons of
   307	its *own* — shutdown, the cap, a policy change — which it does by reclaiming
   308	and recording `:lapsed` with that cause, not by silently declining to count
   309	evidence it received. A renewal creates no obligation to grant *more* time; it
   310	obliges the grantor to honour the time already granted.
   311	
   312	**A renewal is never acknowledged**, so a holder never learns that its evidence
   313	was observed. Two rules follow for the holder, and both are its own discipline
   314	rather than anything the grantor enforces:
   315	
   316	- **Renew at a fraction of the duration.** The sizing relation below asks that
   317	  the duration exceed twice the renewal interval, so an interval of *at most*
   318	  half satisfies it and something under half leaves margin for rate skew. Treat
   319	  the duration as a safety bound, never as a cadence.
   320	- **Stop acting at the bound.** A holder whose own clock has passed the
   321	  duration since its last renewal — **or since it observed the grant, whichever
   322	  is later** — must cease acting on what it holds, whether or not it has heard
   323	  anything. The grant seeds the holder's bound exactly as it seeds the judge's;
   324	  without that, a holder doing short work that never renews would have no bound
   325	  at all. Nothing in this vocabulary excludes a holder that keeps working past
   326	  its bound, and a paused agent that resumes after lapse acts on work the
   327	  grantor has already reclaimed and regranted.
   328	
   329	  This is damage limitation, not exclusion. The two windows do not coincide:
   330	  the judge's opens when *it* grants, the holder's when the grant *reaches* it,
   331	  and the difference is one delivery. A holder can therefore still be inside its
   332	  own bound after the grantor has reclaimed. Where a composition needs genuine
   333	  exclusion it must obtain it from the resource — a fencing token, a
   334	  single-writer medium, whatever the resource itself offers — because a lease
   335	  bounds attention and does not arbitrate access.
   336	
   337	## Attribution is a composition duty
   338	
   339	A renewal is evidence **about its author**, and a grant establishes terms only
   340	because the grantor authored it. Both depend on the judge being able to tell
   341	who wrote a fact — and **DaoStream does not supply that.** Its Concurrency
   342	section contemplates multiple writers and leaves one-log-per-writer to
   343	interpretation; a descriptor carries no authorization; and the attribution a
   344	WebSocket boundary provides is composed envelope data, not a property of the
   345	medium.
   346	
   347	So the composition owes it. A judged medium must give the judge a basis for
   348	authorship — one medium per author, an author key the boundary adds, or
   349	transport attribution as the WebSocket boundary provides.
   350	
   351	Unlike the obligation to judge, this one **is refusable at assembly** — but not
   352	by inspecting DaoStream, which declares surfaces, outcomes and retention and
   353	says nothing about authorship. What the judge's constructor receives, alongside
   354	its medium, is an **attribution resolver**: the composition's own answer to
   355	*who wrote this fact*, whether that is a per-author medium binding, an envelope
   356	key the boundary stamps, or a transport's attachment identity. Assembly refuses
   357	a judge handed no resolver, or one incompatible with the medium it was given,
   358	exactly as the WebSocket boundary refuses a reject-mode destination — and the
   359	thing being inspected is the composition's own supplied evidence, never a
   360	property DaoStream was asked to have. Absent attribution, "only grantor-authored facts establish
   361	terms" is unenforceable: any writer can append an `:accepted` fact naming
   362	itself and take tenure. This is not a theoretical corner. Of the three uses
   363	that justify this vocabulary, only the served connection has attribution by
   364	construction; the forwarder pause and the shared-work claim run over in-process
   365	media where nothing distinguishes writers unless a composition arranges it.
   366	
   367	## Lapse is a conclusion; reclaim is an act
   368	
   369	Nothing fires when a lease lapses. The grantor runs an interpreter that reads
   370	the medium through its own cursor, stamps each fact it observes with the most
   371	recent tick it has seen, and judges: when the interval since its last relevant
   372	observation exceeds the granted duration plus whatever tolerance this judge
   373	allows, the guard has failed. Nothing is traced and nothing is marked — the
   374	judge classifies each lease it already knows about — but the ordering a
   375	collector observes holds for the same reason: conclude about everything before
   376	acting on anything, so that an act cannot change what a later conclusion is
   377	drawn from.
   378	
   379	Anyone may reach that conclusion; only the possessor's conclusion has effect.
   380	The interpreter composed with the resource — the one that can actually close
   381	the socket, stop the forwarder, free the entry — is the one whose judgment
   382	becomes a `:lapsed` fact. Two judges with different tolerances may disagree and
   383	neither is wrong; what settles it is **possession, not truth**, and that is
   384	what reclaiming *without consensus* means.
   385	
   386	**The judge is an interpreter over two streams.** One carries the lease facts.
   387	The other carries **ticks** — plain data appended by a host adapter when a
   388	timer fires, which is the ordinary form the governing document prescribes for
   389	turning a host event into stream data.
   390	
   391	What the tick stream supplies is **time as data**, not a wake-up. DaoStream
   392	invokes nothing and has no readiness extension, so a deposited tick rouses
   393	nobody; a driver still has to call `next`, and that driver is where cadence
   394	comes from. The distinction matters twice over. It keeps the clock out of lease
   395	code — a judge that called a host clock on each observation would be making the
   396	host call this document forbids, so **observation time is the most recent tick
   397	observed before the fact**, and the clock is read only by the adapter that
   398	deposits ticks. And it adds a term to the bound: the driver's own polling lag
   399	over the tick stream, which is why cadence must be declared as the interval
   400	between *completed judgment passes* rather than between timer deposits.
   401	
   402	A tick is a monotonic reading in the same representation a duration uses, and
   403	successive ticks never go backwards; a judge that reads a tick older than one
   404	it has already seen has read a defective fact. Two cursors carry no ordering
   405	between them, so the pass is specified rather than left to the scheduler. At
   406	each judgment pass the judge, in this order:
   407	
   408	1. **Drains the tick cursor to `:dao.stream/blocked`.** The newest tick drained
   409	   is *now* for this entire pass. Without this step time never advances, and a
   410	   pass that omitted it would judge every lease against a stale reading.
   411	2. **Drains the lease-fact cursor to `:dao.stream/blocked`**, stamping every
   412	   fact it finds with that same *now*. A tick judged while an already-deposited
   413	   renewal sits unread is a false lapse manufactured by scheduling; stamping
   414	   with the pass's own reading is what makes the two cursors comparable at all,
   415	   since nothing orders them in the medium. The stamp can only overstate a
   416	   fact's recency — a renewal deposited long ago but read this pass looks
   417	   newer than it was — which errs toward retention, the direction this document
   418	   already chooses.
   419	3. **Judges.** A lease is due for reclaim when its interval since the most
   420	   recent relevant observation exceeds duration plus tolerance, **or** when a

