   436	
   437	**The judge is composed inside the boundary that possesses the resource.** It
   438	closes what it itself holds — the socket its own adapter wraps, the forwarder
   439	its own composition drives — which is the one act the governing document allows
   440	a boundary to take beyond appending. A judge that does not possess the resource
   441	cannot reclaim it, and a composition that wires one is building an advisory
   442	observer, not a grantor: the possessor must append the effect and observe its
   443	outcome as data, and then the record cannot follow the act inside a single
   444	pass. This document specifies the possessing case; the other is a different
   445	design and is not described here.
   446	
   447	**The record goes to the grantor's own log**, not to the holder. In the
   448	canonical case the reclaim act *is* closing the connection, so an append aimed
   449	at the holder would fail every time rather than rarely: the grantor's record
   450	and the holder's signal are different facts on different streams, and the
   451	holder learns what happened from its own medium (for a served connection, an
   452	ordinary `:ws/closed`).
   453	
   454	Even on the grantor's own log the record is evidence that a reclaim was
   455	performed, never a promise that one will be. If the append fails, the resource
   456	is reclaimed and unrecorded; the lease stays in the judge's pending-reclaim set
   457	and the append is retried, and readers must treat the absence of a `:lapsed`
   458	fact as no evidence of continued tenure.
   459	
   460	## Whose clock decides
   461	
   462	**Durations cross boundaries; moments never do.** A grant carries a duration, a
   463	renewal carries nothing, and no expiry timestamp appears in any fact.
   464	
   465	The judge measures intervals between its own observations, so **offset skew
   466	cannot enter the comparison** — it happens entirely on one clock. **Rate skew
   467	remains**: a judge whose clock runs fast relative to the holder's shortens the
   468	real tolerance below what the holder expects, and that is a false lapse with
   469	zero transit delay. The renewal fraction absorbs it, which makes the sizing
   470	relation a skew budget as well as a load budget.
   471	
   472	Transit delay cuts both ways, and conflating them is how tolerance gets sized
   473	to zero. **While a renewal is in flight it protects nothing**: the interval
   474	since the previous observation keeps growing, and a judgment pass inside the
   475	flight window false-lapses a holder that renewed on time. That is what the
   476	tolerance must cover, and why the renewal fraction exists as margin. **Once the
   477	renewal is observed** the window is re-seeded from that later observation, so
   478	the same delay then extends real tenure — safe for the holder, and unsafe for
   479	the scarce resource, since a late-observed renewal from an already-dead holder
   480	buys a fresh duration. The evidence carries no generation or sequence, and its
   481	arrival is its entire meaning. This document takes that trade deliberately,
   482	preferring to extend a dead tenure over reclaiming a live one.
   483	
   484	**Continuity is lost when no eligible renewal is observed before a completed
   485	judgment pass finds the interval past duration plus tolerance.** Partition
   486	length alone does not decide it: a partition longer than the duration can be
   487	survived when tolerance and tick phase cover the excess, and a shorter one can
   488	still be fatal depending on when it begins relative to the last observation. No
   489	discipline available here prevents the fatal case — a `:dao.stream/ok` on an
   490	outbound path implies nothing about delivery — and the holder's remedy is
   491	renegotiation when it reconnects.
   492	
   493	Sizing is stated as relations, never numbers, because the numbers belong to
   494	compositions: the medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag; the
   495	tolerance covers expected flight time and rate skew; the granted duration
   496	exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.
   497	
   498	## Judging lapse
   499	
   500	The judge keeps, as private state, for every lease it has granted:
   501	
   502	- the **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;
   503	- the **tenure start**, the observation of the grant, which `:dao.lease/max` is
   504	  measured from and which no renewal overwrites;
   505	- the observation of the **most recent relevant fact**, the grant or the newest
   506	  renewal, whichever is later, which the duration is measured from;
   507	- a **pending-reclaim set**, the leases reclaimed whose `:lapsed` record has not
   508	  yet been appended.
   509	
   510	**A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**
   511	That is what the holder field is for, and where the attribution duty is spent:
   512	a renewal from anyone else is a fact about someone else, and the judge measures
   513	silence as though it had not arrived. For a grant answering a proposal, the
   514	holder is the proposal's author; for an unsolicited grant, it is the party the
   515	grant was delivered to — the attachment, in the served-connection case — and
   516	the grantor records which when it grants, because nothing later can recover it.
   517	
   518	This is the grantor's **ledger of live grants**, and it is not the registry the
   519	invariants forbid: a registry records who is *observing* a stream, while this
   520	records what its owner has *given out* — the grantor's own obligations, private
   521	to it, consulted by nothing else. (It is not a root set in the collector's
   522	sense either. Roots are where a trace *starts*; these are the things whose
   523	liveness is in question, which under the mapping below makes them the
   524	candidates, not the roots.)
   525	
   526	**A lease leaves the ledger when its reclaim is performed and its terminal
   527	record is on the medium** — `:lapsed` appended, or the reclaim that answered a
   528	release completed and recorded. Until both, it stays, which is what the
   529	pending-reclaim set is for. Without an exit rule the ledger grows without
   530	bound, which would put the leak in the one component this document exists to
   531	keep bounded.
   532	
   533	Seeding from the grant is what makes a never-renewed lease judgeable. A holder
   534	that accepts a grant and dies before its first renewal supplies no renewal to
   535	measure from, and without this rule such a lease could never lapse — the exact
   536	leak this document exists to prevent, in its most likely form. Note where the
   537	seed comes from: the grantor's facts and the holder's need not share a medium,
   538	and in the served-connection case they do not — the grant goes to the grantor's
   539	own log while renewals arrive on the deposit medium. **The grantor seeds from
   540	its own act of granting**, not from reading the grant back.
   541	
   542	**This state is not rebuildable from the stream.** Renewals carry no time, so
   543	observation times are judge-local facts appearing nowhere in the medium. The
   544	terms are worse than volatile: `:accepted` is an ordinary stream element under
   545	ordinary retention, so it can be evicted while the lease is live, leaving a
   546	restarting judge reading renewals for a lease whose terms it cannot recover.
   547	A composition therefore either **persists the terms** — the judge's own root
   548	set, written where its own restart can read it — or **re-grants on restart**,
   549	below. Restating terms on the medium is not a third option: statuses are
   550	authored once, and a second `:accepted` is a new lease rather than a restatement
   551	of an old one.
   552	
   553	**Absence is evidence only over a window the judge actually observed.** A false
   554	lapse has no deopt, so the discipline is conservative:
   555	
   556	- **A gap is not silence.** After any `:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no
   557	  renewal in the retained tail is **unknown**, and the one-duration rule below
   558	  applies. Do not infer that a surviving older renewal is the newest: eviction
   559	  order is the medium's declared nature and this contract standardizes none, so
   560	  under a medium that evicts arbitrarily a newer renewal may be gone while an
   561	  older one remains, inflating the measured interval and false-lapsing a live
   562	  holder. A judged medium declares evict-oldest for the same reason a deposit
   563	  destination must, and is refused at assembly where it does not; the gap rule
   564	  above is what remains safe on a medium whose order is merely unknown.
   565	- **After losing its state** — a restart, or a gap spanning the watched window
   566	  — a judge knows nothing about silence. The honest state is **unknown**, not
   567	  lapsed: "no renewal was found" and "no renewal was observed" are different
   568	  facts, and collapsing them is how a restart reclaims a live holder's grant.
   569	  The judge observes for one full duration from resumed observation before
   570	  concluding lapse.
   571	- **A restarting grantor may instead reclaim and re-grant.** For every resource
   572	  it still possesses it performs the reclaim first — ending whatever tenure
   573	  existed, by the same idempotent act any lapse would use — and only then
   574	  appends a fresh unsolicited `:accepted`. The reclaim is what ends the old
   575	  lease; a grant naming a new identity says nothing about an old one, and
   576	  skipping the reclaim leaves a previous holder acting on a resource that has
   577	  been given away.
   578	
   579	  This recovery is not free of history, and the earlier draft's claim that it
   580	  was is withdrawn. To reclaim and re-grant, a grantor must know *what it
   581	  possesses* — the resource, how to reclaim it, and to whom the new grant goes.
   582	  That inventory is the resource's own, not the lease's: an endpoint knows its
   583	  open connections, a composition knows the forwarders it wired. Where no such
   584	  inventory survives the restart, the grantor cannot identify what it once
   585	  granted and this recovery is unavailable; persisting the terms is then the
   586	  only option.
   587	
   588	  The new grant carries a new identity, so the old lease's record is left
   589	  **orphaned** on the medium: a live-looking `:accepted` with no terminal fact,
   590	  which the grantor cannot lapse because it no longer knows the id. That is a
   591	  consequence of the recovery, not a defect in it, and it is why the reader
   592	  rule is general — *the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is never evidence of
   593	  tenure*, for orphaned records exactly as for failed appends. Where the old
   594	  identity is recoverable after all, from persisted terms or a retained tail,
   595	  the grantor appends `:lapsed` with cause and the record closes properly.
   596	
   597	Three limits of the promise follow, and each must be stated rather than left to
   598	be discovered. `:dao.lease/max` caps consecutive tenure **within one
   599	uninterrupted judge lifetime**; every restart resets the measurement, so a judge
   600	that restarts more often than one duration can extend a supposedly capped lease
   601	indefinitely. A grantor whose restarts outpace one duration never completes an
   602	observation window at all, so it leaks continuously while every component
   603	reports healthy. And **a medium that gaps more often than one duration leaks the
   604	same way without any restart at all** — on a shared deposit medium one flooding
   605	attachment evicts everyone's renewals, every pass sees a gap, every window
   606	restarts, and no dead holder on that medium ever lapses. Per-attachment media
   607	are the isolation. In all three cases `:dao.lease/max` still bounds tenure,
   608	because tenure start is set once at the grant and no gap or restart moves it.
   609	
   610	## Whose obligation
   611	
   612	A grantor that never judges leaks its resource without bound. That obligation
   613	cannot be a callback and cannot be a component, so it lives in two places.
   614	
   615	**Possession.** The leak lands on the grantor's own resource, so the party with
   616	the incentive is the party with the authority. For a resource that dies with
   617	its process — a socket, an in-memory table — a grantor that stops running holds
   618	nothing anyone can reach, and the obligation ends with it. **This does not hold
   619	for durable resources.** A reserved slot on disk or a claim on a persistent log
   620	outlives its grantor, and a grantor's death then leaves the grant held with no
   621	judge running — precisely the stuck state this document exists to end.
   622	
   623	A lease over a durable resource therefore requires more than this vocabulary
   624	supplies, and a composition that has not settled all three should keep its
   625	leases process-scoped: a judge whose durability matches the resource's, so the
   626	root set survives what the process does not; a stated rule for **which
   627	incarnation may reclaim**, since two judges believing themselves authoritative
   628	over one durable resource will both reclaim it; and a **fencing mechanism from
   629	the resource itself**, because a reclaim that a previous incarnation can still
   630	undo is not a reclaim. None of these is a lease concept, and this document does

