   631	not invent them.
   632	
   633	**Declaration.** A composition that grants leases declares its judgment cadence
   634	and wires three things that realize it: the tick stream, the judging
   635	interpreter, and **the runtime that drives that interpreter**. The third is
   636	easy to omit and fatal to omit — a composition with ticks and no driver
   637	satisfies every prohibition in this document and still leaks, because the timer
   638	fires, the adapter appends, and nothing advances the judge. Failing to wire it
   639	is a defect, and it is honest to say that nothing can refuse it.
   640	The WebSocket boundary can reject a reject-mode deposit destination because a
   641	constructor inspects a destination's declared nature; there is no counterpart
   642	here, because no constructor can inspect whether a composition will later
   643	append an `:accepted` fact. This is a defect detectable by review, with no
   644	assembly-time or runtime signature, and the document claims no enforcement it
   645	does not have.
   646	
   647	The cadence being declared is the **maximum interval between completed
   648	judgment passes**, not the interval at which ticks are deposited. A tick that
   649	arrives while nothing drains it has not produced a judgment, so a promise
   650	resting on deposit intervals would be measuring the wrong thing.
   651	
   652	What a grantor can promise, it promises about itself: **a grantor completing
   653	judgment passes at its declared cadence reclaims every lapsed lease within
   654	duration plus tolerance plus cadence, plus the cost of the reclaim itself** —
   655	conditional on completing an uninterrupted observation window, per the restart
   656	limits above.
   657	
   658	## What a lease may never do
   659	
   660	- **Never a live object.** Everything one can do about a lease is append a fact
   661	  or judge one.
   662	- **Never fires into lease code.** No timer holds a callback and no
   663	  notification marks a lapse; a tick is data on a stream like any other event,
   664	  and a composition that wants to observe reclaims reads the medium like any
   665	  other interpreter.
   666	- **Never a registry.** A shared lease table is hidden global state. A
   667	  grantor's own root set is not one: it records what its owner has given out,
   668	  it is private to that owner, and where it must survive a restart it is
   669	  persisted as a stream that owner writes and reads — data like everything
   670	  else, and nothing anyone else consults.
   671	- **Never puts absolute time on the wire.**
   672	- **Never erases.** A reclaim frees the resource, not the record; the medium's
   673	  retention policy is the only eraser, here as everywhere.
   674	- **Never renewed by machinery.** A holder renews from its own control flow,
   675	  because renewal is evidence the holder is alive.
   676	- **No rule here corresponds to the invariant against collapsing
   677	  interpretation and execution**, and the omission is deliberate rather than an
   678	  oversight. Judging and reclaiming are one interpreter by design: the party
   679	  that concludes is the party that possesses, exactly as the precedent's VM
   680	  evaluates its own guard and deopts, and as a boundary adapter closes the
   681	  socket it itself holds. The invariant governs layers, and no layer here
   682	  interprets what it also executes — the stream does neither. What the
   683	  invariant does forbid is stated positively instead: anyone may judge, and
   684	  only the possessor's judgment has effect.
   685	- **Never touches retention.** This is the load-bearing denial: a lease may not
   686	  extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no DaoStream operation
   687	  may consult a lease. `dao.stream.md` is explicit that eviction never waits on
   688	  a reader, and a lease that could hold a stream open would make one stalled
   689	  holder every other reader's unbounded growth.
   690	
   691	## Renewal forever
   692	
   693	A hostile holder gains little by renewing forever, and what it gains is priced.
   694	Renewal buys tolerance for silence and nothing else. Renewing forever holds the
   695	lease forever, which `:dao.lease/max` bounds within a judge lifetime — past it
   696	the grantor lapses regardless of renewals, and an honest long-lived holder
   697	proposes anew. Renewal costs presence, which is the honest price, and a holder
   698	able to pay it indefinitely is indistinguishable from a legitimately long-lived
   699	one; distinguishing them is admission policy — who is granted anything at all —
   700	never lease vocabulary.
   701	
   702	Whether a leased grant is dangerous is judged where it is granted. A paused
   703	forwarder retains nothing extra, so that pause harms only the pauser; a lease
   704	over a genuinely scarce resource needs its cap. The vocabulary carries the cap;
   705	the composition decides it.
   706	
   707	## Delegated renewal
   708	
   709	A renewal is evidence about its author. A party emitting evidence about
   710	*another* party is delegation, and delegation is **out of scope here** — denied
   711	now rather than deferred vaguely, with the conditions for revisiting stated so
   712	the judgment is auditable.
   713	
   714	An agent that renews on a holder's behalf without holding evidence about that
   715	holder is Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` in cleaner materials, manufacturing
   716	false liveness for a wedged holder. An agent that renews *on evidence* needs
   717	its own liveness signal from the holder — which is a lease — so the honest
   718	structure is a chain of ordinary leases, each link its own grantor and
   719	possessor, terminating at whoever is doing the work.
   720	
   721	Such a chain is safe but not meaningful by default. The terminal grantor is
   722	never unsafe: it reclaims when its immediate holder goes quiet, and that local
   723	rule is unchanged by anything below it, which it cannot even see. What a chain
   724	changes is what those renewals *mean*, and that is a property of each
   725	intermediate renewer's composition, checkable only by reading it:
   726	
   727	> For each link, the renewer's predicate is *I append a renewal only while I
   728	> hold fresh evidence, observed on my own clock, that my immediate holder is
   729	> still there.* The end-to-end meaning — that the terminal grantor's reclaim
   730	> tracks the worker's death — holds only if that predicate is true at **every**
   731	> link. Any weaker answer is not a weakening but a **cut**: from that point
   732	> outward, renewals prove only the renewer's liveness.
   733	
   734	Neither benefit usually claimed for delegation survives inspection. Batching
   735	amortized a remote call in Jini; here N renewals to one grantor are N appends
   736	the judge already reads in one pass, cross-grantor batching is impossible by
   737	construction, and coalescing frames on a wire is carriage the transport owns.
   738	Cadence decoupling is worse than the alternative: the terminal grantor cannot
   739	reclaim faster than the innermost sub-lease permits, so a chain is strictly
   740	worse than granting the loose duration directly. Each link also adds its own
   741	duration and cadence to detection latency, and a dead manager fells every
   742	sub-holder beneath it.
   743	
   744	What delegation genuinely offers — one accountable counterparty, with admission
   745	and sub-allocation a link down — needs no delegation at all: the manager is
   746	simply **holder of record**. It holds the grantor's lease, sub-leases its own
   747	attention beneath it, and the grantor sees one holder and is told nothing about
   748	the rest. That shape is ordinary composition and is legal today.
   749	
   750	Should a real need for third-party renewal arrive, the weakest sufficient
   751	mechanism is a holder-authored fact designating acceptable renewers, with the
   752	judge counting a renewal only from the holder or a designated renewer. It
   753	presupposes exactly what *Attribution is a composition duty* already requires,
   754	and nothing more. Accepting any renewal that merely names a lease is not a
   755	weaker mechanism but the absence of one — tolerable only where the medium's own
   756	admission already restricts writers, which is designation done implicitly and
   757	should be named as such rather than adopted as a design. Depth beyond one
   758	delegation would need chained, attenuable, offline-verifiable authorization,
   759	which is a different problem and not this document's.
   760	
   761	## Out of scope
   762	
   763	What is leasable is the domain's business; this document standardizes
   764	vocabulary and duties, and refuses all policy.
   765	
   766	**Carriage** — how these facts cross a particular boundary — belongs to the
   767	transport documents. Renewals ride as ordinary payload values, which is the
   768	reading consistent with a transport that never learns lease vocabulary. A
   769	`:lapsed` fact does not cross at all: it is the grantor's own record on its own
   770	log, and in the canonical case the reclaim has already closed the channel that
   771	would have carried it. What reaches a remote holder is the reclaim itself,
   772	observed on the holder's own medium.
   773	
   774	This document deliberately assigns **no close code**. A reclaimed WebSocket
   775	connection surfaces to its client as an ordinary `:ws/closed`, and reattaching
   776	yields a fresh unsolicited grant. The distinguishing close code
   777	`dao.stream.ws.md` defines means something else — the served stream *ended*, so
   778	the client must stop and not reattach — and a reclaimed attachment is not an
   779	ended stream. Where a served stream is shared between attachments, reclaiming
   780	one lease cannot end it, and the transport defines no per-attachment code.
   781	Should reclaim ever need to be distinguishable on the wire, that is an item for
   782	the transport's own deferred close-code design, raised there rather than
   783	settled here.
   784	
   785	`dao.stream.ws.md` also defers liveness probing, and its Deferred list awaits a
   786	durable home for a pause vocabulary. Both are lease-shaped, and this document
   787	is the plausible home for their semantics; neither is claimed here until the
   788	transport's wire-contract gate settles what crosses the wire.
   789	
   790	**Cancellation** is not out of scope — it is what a lapse *is*, and the
   791	generalization is worth stating because it is easy to miss. To cancel something
   792	ongoing is to stop wanting it continued, and a holder that stops renewing has
   793	said exactly that. The lease form is stronger than a cancellation flag in the
   794	case that matters most: a flag needs its setter alive to set it, while a lease
   795	cancels when the canceller dies, hangs, or is partitioned away. Where the thing
   796	being cancelled is itself a lease holder — an interpreter that checks its own
   797	tenure at its own points, in the way this document already asks holders to stop
   798	acting at their bound — non-renewal is cooperative cancellation with a deadline
   799	attached, and it needs no separate mechanism.
   800	
   801	What a lease cannot do is **retract what already happened**. It ends
   802	continuation, not consequence: an effect already produced stays produced, a
   803	message already sent stays sent, and a computation that finished before the
   804	lapse finished. That is the honest boundary — a lease cancels the *future* of
   805	an activity and never its past — and it is also why a lease is the wrong
   806	instrument for a one-shot answer that will never arrive, where there is no
   807	continuation to end.
   808	
   809	**Transfer of a lease between holders**, and **authorization of who may be
   810	granted anything at all**, wait for a real need.
   811	
   812	Three uses justify this vocabulary: the pause a reader holds over a forwarder,
   813	the lifetime of a served connection, and a claim an agent leaves on shared
   814	work. Three is enough for a small vocabulary; it is not a reason to make every
   815	timeout, cancellation, or lock a lease.

