   159	
   160	Nothing accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket inbox would
   161	be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed, sized by
   162	nobody. Retention lives in the medium the boundary deposits into, composed
   163	and sized by the host.
   164	
   165	There are no callback hooks — no `on-open!` / `on-message!` / `on-close!`
   166	surface of the kind the host's own socket API offers. Every socket event
   167	becomes data on the deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)
   168	alike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether
   169	anyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.
   170	
   171	### Serving
   172	
   173	Serving is the mirror of attaching, and the deposit model is the same on both
   174	ends: each accepted connection's inbound messages are deposited — enveloped
   175	with that connection's attachment identity, exactly as on the client — onto a
   176	stream the host composition wired.
   177	
   178	**That stream is not the served stream.** A client's `append!` is a request to
   179	the server, never a broadcast: what a client writes lands in a medium only the
   180	serving host's own interpreters read. Nothing a client sends reaches the other
   181	attachments by default.
   182	
   183	The alternative — depositing a client's messages straight into the served
   184	stream — would make every client a writer to the stream every other client is
   185	reading, by nothing more than holding a handle. A composition that wants that
   186	writes it explicitly: an interpreter reads the request medium, decides what
   187	qualifies, and appends to the served stream. Then the admission rule is a
   188	piece of code someone wrote, in one place, rather than a consequence of the
   189	transport's shape.
   190	
   191	### Ending a served stream
   192	
   193	Closing a served stream is its owner's `close!`, and connected clients have to
   194	be able to tell that from a connection that merely dropped. The two call for
   195	opposite responses — a dropped connection means reattach, an ended stream
   196	means stop — and a client has no reader surface, so the answer can only reach
   197	it as a deposited event.
   198	
   199	The closing frame carries it. When the serving host closes a stream it closes
   200	each attached connection **with a distinguishing close code**, rather than
   201	dropping the socket. The client's adapter reads that code and deposits
   202	`:ws/ended` instead of `:ws/closed`. (The exact code, in the protocol's
   203	application-defined range, is settled with the handshake wire format; see
   204	Deferred.)
   205	
   206	What separates the two cases is not the closing but whether anything was
   207	heard. A connection lost to a network fault delivers no closing frame at all,
   208	and the host reports that distinctly — the client learns that nobody told it
   209	anything, and deposits `:ws/closed`.
   210	
   211	Three outcomes follow, and the third is the honest limit:
   212	
   213	- **A closing frame arrived** — the stream ended. Stop; there is nothing to
   214	  reattach to.
   215	- **Nothing arrived** — reattach. If the stream is in fact gone, the handshake
   216	  answers with the authoritative disclaimer and the client learns on that
   217	  attempt instead of this one. A frame lost in transit costs one wasted
   218	  reattach, never a wrong conclusion the client cannot escape.
   219	- **The server cannot be reached at all** — unknowable. Nothing can distinguish
   220	  a crashed peer from an unreachable one, so a client keeps retrying, which is
   221	  correct: the stream may still be there. Bounding those retries is the
   222	  consumer's policy, not this transport's.
   223	
   224	The close code is the fast path for the common case — a healthy server ending a
   225	stream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case
   226	resolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.
   227	
   228	### Deposit Admission
   229	
   230	The adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and
   231	cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the
   232	deposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced
   233	where it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:
   234	
   235	- The destination must declare **evict-oldest retention with a
   236	  host-declared capacity**. It never answers `:dao.stream/full`; under
   237	  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one
   238	  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an
   239	  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the
   240	  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed
   241	  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting
   242	  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,
   243	  like any storage failure.)
   244	- The destination must also be able to **carry every envelope this transport
   245	  deposits** — attachment identifiers, lifecycle events, and every value the
   246	  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers
   247	  `:dao.stream/invalid-value` on some envelope is as unwired as a reject-mode
   248	  one; retention mode alone is not admission.
   249	- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused
   250	  when the boundary is composed. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before
   251	  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.
   252	- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit
   253	  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is
   254	  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination
   255	  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;
   256	  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary
   257	  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.
   258	  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the
   259	  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and
   260	  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary
   261	  `attach!`.
   262	
   263	Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps
   264	appear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Whether
   265	that window can be resized after the fact is the destination transport's
   266	declared nature, not this transport's business. An adaptive policy — grow on
   267	rising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own
   268	telemetry, not a transport behavior.
   269	
   270	The accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention
   271	pressure surfaces as reader-visible `gap`s, and a dead destination tears the
   272	attachment down. But a deposit that fails is a deposit that did not land, and
   273	neither can the teardown event land in the destination that just refused it —
   274	so in that one case the local failure is observable only through the deposit
   275	result the adapter holds and through subsequent handle operations, and the
   276	peer observes it only if teardown reaches the wire.
   277	
   278	## Envelope
   279	
   280	Transport-owned keys, qualified under `:ws/…`. The exact key set is TBD

